<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.developer.family-controls</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- Sign in with Apple. Needed before a Contract Envelope can exist, because
	     an envelope hangs off an account. Adding this entitlement means the App
	     ID needs the matching capability enabled in Xcode's Signing &
	     Capabilities, exactly as Family Controls did — until then a device
	     build fails to sign. See app/README.md. -->
	<key>com.apple.developer.applesignin</key>
	<array>
		<string>Default</string>
	</array>
	<!-- HealthKit, read-only in practice: the importer asks for workouts and
	     nothing else, and never writes. Same capability caveat as the others —
	     the App ID needs HealthKit enabled under Signing & Capabilities or a
	     device build fails to sign. -->
	<key>com.apple.developer.healthkit</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- The ledger's backup, in the user's own iCloud (Store/LedgerMirror.swift).
	     Deleting the app used to clear every commitment and all debt, which was
	     the cheapest way out of a deal in the product.

	     CloudKit rather than our own backend on purpose: the ledger carries the
	     requirement, the restriction profile and every imported workout, none of
	     which the server has ever been allowed to see, and "Health data never
	     leaves the phone" is a promise made to testers. A private database keeps
	     it — the copy goes from the user's phone to the user's iCloud, and
	     Earned's servers are not party to it.

	     The App ID needs iCloud enabled with CloudKit and this container, or a
	     device build fails to sign. -->
	<key>com.apple.developer.icloud-services</key>
	<array>
		<string>CloudKit</string>
	</array>
	<key>com.apple.developer.icloud-container-identifiers</key>
	<array>
		<string>iCloud.com.pattheratch.earned</string>
	</array>
	<!-- Remote notifications, for the three actionable asks another person can
	     make of this user: a shared-commitment invitation, an accountability
	     partner request, and an override approval. Never social engagement —
	     the outbox's `kind` column is a three-value check constraint, so the
	     vocabulary is the allow-list (docs/social-architecture.md §9).

	     Set per configuration rather than hardcoded: `development` for Debug,
	     `production` for Release (see APS_ENVIRONMENT in project.yml). The two
	     are separate APNs environments with separate hosts, and a token minted
	     for one is refused by the other as BadDeviceToken — which arrives as
	     silent non-delivery and looks exactly like a bug in the app. Shipping
	     a TestFlight build with `development` baked in would have registered
	     sandbox tokens for every tester and delivered nothing to any of them.

	     Same capability caveat as the others: the App ID needs Push
	     Notifications enabled under Signing & Capabilities, or a device build
	     fails to sign. -->
	<key>aps-environment</key>
	<string>$(APS_ENVIRONMENT)</string>
	<!-- Shared with the DeviceActivityMonitor extension, which is a separate
	     process and cannot see this app's Documents directory. It carries the
	     shield plan and nothing else — the ledger deliberately stays put. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.application-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>group.com.pattheratch.earned</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
